## Smoke test 1 — cobblestone-qemu

**What it proves:** run Codex on real x86 and tell me what came out. Four
subjects through the seed under QEMU, then four checkers on fib.

### Raw U57 first, to see the failure

Both our defects are live at `49fa9f27`, measured rather than inferred:

    fib       BUILT  0m07s          the transport works, the seed boots
    zigemit   RED    zigemit.zig:3891:9: error: local variable shadows
                                     declaration of 'cx_new'

`codexir` was NOT run at raw U57. It emits the same prelude and would fail on
the identical line, so the run would cost five minutes to confirm arithmetic.
The build stops at the first red by design and there is nothing to learn from
proving a shared failure twice.

### u58-candidate, and there is no third defect

    ring plug   0m26s      7442 lines, 390080 bytes
    fib         0m07s
    zigemit     0m41s
    codexir     4m35s      (compile 2m46s, transpile 1m44s)
    x86emit     6m26s      (compile 3m06s, transpile 3m14s)

    verify zig    PASS   native codexir | zigemit: fib prints 55 and 610
    verify qemu   PASS   bare metal: IR identical to the native tool
    verify x86    PASS   emitted machine code runs
    verify boot   PASS   emitted x86-64 BOOTS and prints 6765
    GREEN 4/4

**The two fixes are sufficient at U57 exactly as they were at U56.**

### The ring plug is byte-identical across the two releases

`ringplug-source.codex` is 390564 bytes at BOTH `u57-candidate` (over U56) and
`u58-candidate` (over U57), with the same source list and no diff. Every
chapter it bundles -- `Core/Name`, `Core/SourceText`, `Types/CodexType`,
`Ast/AstNodes`, `IR/IRChapter`, `plugs/common/PlugTypes`,
`plugs/common/IRTextParser`, and the Zig Emitter -- is untouched by U57.

So the plug is literally the same program at both pins, and any difference
between the two runs is the SEED's. Corroborated by the compile diagnostics
being identical too: 26 CDX4010, 4 CDX3005, 1 CDX4030 at each.

### Timings say nothing moved structurally

    stage              U56+ours   U57+ours
    ring plug             0m25s      0m26s
    zigemit compile       0m25s      0m26s
    codexir compile       2m39s      2m46s
    codexir transpile     1m43s      1m44s
    x86emit compile       2m54s      3m06s
    x86emit transpile     3m14s      3m14s

Every stage within a few percent, consistent with a seed that grew from
3,217,563 to 3,322,781 bytes. **U57 is marginally SLOWER, not faster** -- the
first reading of this table compared U57's compile step against U56's subject
TOTAL and read as a 40% improvement that does not exist.

### The x86 change is real and invisible to fib, which is correct

The emitted x86 for fib is byte-identical at both pins: 287,765 bytes of dump,
26 symbols, 89,677 reassembled. A difference was registered as EXPECTED before
the run because `Emit/X86_64*.codex` moved; there is none, and the reason is
specific rather than a shrug.

The change is COMPILER-20's over-application miscompile:

    -    is FunTy (pt) (row) (rt) -> emit-partial-application st name [] (1 + fun-type-arity rt)
    +     let ua = lookup-x86-arity (st.user-arities) name
    +     in emit-partial-application st name [] (if ua > 0 then ua else 1 + fun-type-arity rt)

plus `emit-over-push-extras`, `emit-trampoline-over-dynamic` and
`emit-over-apply-loop` in `X86_64Compound.codex`, and a new test
`codex/test/ops/saturated-call-returning-function.codex`. **fib applies nothing
over its arity**, so identical bytes is the right answer.

COMPILER-20 was our PR 90, and over-application is a lane we opened (PRs 79, 80,
83, 86). The four fib checkers assert fib's ANSWER, not emitted bytes, so this
arm cannot regress on a legitimate back-end change -- and equally cannot see one.

## Two gaps in our own tooling, found by using it

**The .pyc guard covered two ways in and not the third.** Running
`python3 zig_plug_pages.py` by hand -- which is how `bundle_ringplug.ps1` calls
it -- wrote `roots.cpython-312.pyc` into the checkout. `-B` in a shebang applies
to `./name.py` alone and `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` in `build.sh` covers only
what build.sh spawns, so every `.ps1` call was already outside the guarantee.
The empty `.gitignore` surfaced it in one `git status`, which is what it is for.

The four verifiers already set `sys.dont_write_bytecode` before importing, with
the reason written out; the four scripts that import a local module did not.
They do now, so the guarantee no longer depends on how python was started.

**`run_all.py` could not check a sandbox it did not build.** The checkers read
`$SANDBOX` and always could, but the orchestrator coupled build and check, so a
`build.sh` run had no path to a verdict -- and a sandbox with no RESULT is
documented here as broken. `SANDBOX` naming a directory that already has a
PROVENANCE now means check that one, the same contract `build.sh` already uses.
The verdict above took 10 seconds instead of a 13-minute rebuild.

RESULT now also names the CHECKER sha, because PROVENANCE stamps the qemu sha
at BUILD time and a check-only run can be a different one:

    codex     8570fba1...    branch u58-candidate
    verdict   GREEN          checkers 99aea4593ea9
    built     not by this run

## Smoke test 2 — codex-zig-transpiler

**What it proves, and it is a different question:** the emitter emits the same
bytes for its own source whether it runs on bare metal under QEMU or as the
native binary that run produced. The subject is the whole compiler, so a
rejected or mis-applied PR shows here as a broken invariant rather than a
missing file.

Run on **`master`**, repinned to `cobblestone-u58`. No branch: branches off
Cobblestone are load-bearing, branches off our own repos are drift.

    fixed point   HOLDS, byte-identical      2,959,750 bytes both passes
    arith.codex   MATCHES arith.expected     all 9 lines
    subject       72,906 lines, 3,487,974 bytes   (was 72,741 at U56)
    codexzig.ir   12,167,592 bytes                (was 12,109,027)
    cold build    494s, three guests              (was 481s)

**Stage 6 is the one that matters.** `zig build-exe` succeeds here and fails at
both raw U56 and raw U57 on the same two prelude defects. Nothing upstream runs
that stage, which is why the defects survive Updates.

### One measurement fell out for free

`generated/intake/ringplug-source.codex.blob` is UNCHANGED across the repin --
git says so, and cobblestone-qemu's independent bundler agrees. So the plug
SOURCE is identical at both pins while the compiled kernel is not:

    ringplug.cdx   542,781 -> 590,117 bytes   +8.7%

**Identical input, one variable moved: the seed.** That is the cleanest shape
a comparison here can take, and it arrived without being designed. U57 adds
over-application emission to the x86 back end (COMPILER-20, three new
definitions in `X86_64Compound.codex`), so growth is the expected direction;
the magnitude is unexplained and is an observation, not a finding.

### What this build did NOT repin

The Rust arm's oracles -- `generated/local/{codexir,codexcheck}` and
`generated/codexcheck-subject.codex` -- still date from `e941ba11`.
`build.py` does not move them, `build_codexir.py` does, and `PROVENANCE` says
in its own words that it does not vouch for their pin. Repinning the Rust arm
stays a deliberate act.

### A near-miss worth recording

The two repos' ring plug bundles differ by exactly 2 bytes (390,562 here,
390,564 in cobblestone-qemu). The cause is a comment in each repo's own copy of
`ZigPlugRing.codex` naming its own host-side driver -- `guest.py` here,
`ring_compile.py` there. **Both are correct.** This was nearly filed as a stale
reference on the strength of the name alone; `guest.py` exists in this repo.

The fact that survives is narrower and is about what may be compared: the two
repos build ring plugs from sources that differ by two bytes of prose, so their
ring plug ARTIFACTS are not cross-comparable byte for byte, and `cce.py` and
`BootPaintStubs.codex` are exact duplicates across the two repos that nothing
keeps in step.

## Smoke test 3 — codex-wasm-transpiler, and squaring what was owed

**What it proves:** the module compiles the source that produced it and gets
itself back byte for byte, with two independent roads required to agree.

### The stale-module guard did its job on the way in

Repinning to `cobblestone-u58` made the tracked module (built at `e941ba11`)
older than the checkout, and the self road refused rather than comparing two
emitters and calling the difference a defect. Generation 1 was regenerated
through the zig road first, exactly as the refusal said to.

    zig road      8,287,746 bytes, sha 6223e82a44d53ee9
    self road     agrees BYTE FOR BYTE, 1655.6 MB, 19.4s
    fixed point   IDENTICAL, 8,287,746 bytes both ways
    sample        MATCHES arith.expected, all 9 lines
    subject       72,183 lines, 3,441,876 bytes (was 3,432,921)

The subject grew 8,955 bytes; codex-zig-transpiler's grew 8,937 across the same
repin. Both carry the whole compiler, so they move together, and that they do
is a cheap cross-check on both bundlers.

### The zig road borrowed an emitter and nobody checked its pin

The self road refuses a module built at another revision. **The zig road
reaches the identical hazard through `$CODEXZIG` and was covered by nothing.**
PROVENANCE recorded which binary ran and NOTEd that its provenance belonged to
another repository -- honest, and not a gate.

`borrowed_pin` now reads the receipt that repository writes beside the binary
and refuses a mismatch. An unreadable receipt is reported in preflight and
recorded as a NOTE rather than read as agreement: a binary from elsewhere
cannot be made to say where it came from, and treating silence as a match is
the failure being eliminated.

**Proven to fire before it was trusted.** Pointed at `cobblestone-u57` with the
u58-built binary it refuses in 0.2s, before any work. Both pins are now stated
in preflight rather than assumed:

    codexzig   built at 8570fba1, matching the checkout
    module     generation 1 was built at 8570fba1, matching the checkout

### The bank was a number that made the output look measured

    old bank   2,286,092,288 bytes   2180.2 MB
    peak       1,736,048,640 bytes   1655.6 MB

Every run reported **-24% and read as good news while gating nothing.** At a 2%
ratchet that bank could only have fired on a 34% regression. It was taken
before a memory improvement and never re-taken.

Rebanked at 1655.6 MB, deliberately, at `u58-candidate`, after both roads had
agreed at that pin. The peak was 1655.5 MB at `e941ba11` and 1655.6 MB here, so
it is a number that has held across an Update rather than one run's reading.
The ratchet now fires above 1688.7 MB.

### Both gates are now KNOWN armed

Not assumed, and this is the point of running them:

    --prove-gate           flips one byte of generation 2. Caught at offset
                           4,143,873, reported DIFFERS, restored. FIRES.
    CXWASM_DRIFT_PCT=-1    makes the ratchet refuse a run that grew +0.00%.
                           Exits non-zero. FIRES.

**Two memory files disagreed about whether `--prove-gate` had ever been run** --
the wasm project memory said it had and fires, the handoff memory said it never
had. Neither is evidence now: it was run here and it fires.

### A baseline may only come from a run that passed

`--rebank` wrote `MEMORY.bank` inside `check_memory`, before the fixed point
had been compared -- and before the checkout-moved check whose own message
promises that a build which loses its tree writes nothing. The number is now
held until `main()` has a verdict.

### A tracked file whose content was a function of the FLAG

`generated/codexwasm.diag` is the emitter's stdout for whichever road ran. The
native binary prints CX-DECK deck telemetry and the wasm module does not, so
the two roads write 396 lines and 3 lines into the same tracked path, and
choosing a different flag showed up in `git diff generated/` as a 396-line
change to the artifact set.

Nothing outside `build.py` reads it, `refuse_halt` reads it within the run, and
generation 2's equivalent was already in the gitignored `local/`. Moved beside
it and untracked -- the inconsistency was gen 1's, not a decision.

### Still not built, and honestly labelled

`--road guest` -- the seed compiling the subject and the wasm plug emitting
from it, both on bare metal -- remains NOT BUILT. The README says so in those
words. It is the road that would make the genesis zig-free; `docs/` carries the
argument. Designed, not built, and not pretending otherwise.

### And then the flag was deleted

Running `--prove-gate` once discharged an owed item and proved nothing about
the next thousand builds. Steve's objection is the right one: **a thing kept on
life support because it was once useful is a tax**, and this repository already
says so in `build.py` about a gate it deleted -- "an item that stops catching
anything is a tax".

The check itself is not the tax. What it rules out is not a broken `==` but the
comparison pointed at the WRONG OPERAND, after which every run agrees forever
and the byte count printed beside IDENTICAL still looks right. Nothing else
here catches that, and a green run cannot show it.

So the flag is gone and the self-test runs every build. Measured rather than
estimated: **43 ms against a 94-second build.**

Both the self-test and the real comparison now read through one `operands`
expression, so a refactor cannot move one without the other. The first attempt
read `GEN2_WAT` directly inside the self-test, which would have proved only
that the function can open a file -- a weaker check than the flag it replaced,
and caught before it was committed.

**Proven to fire before being trusted.** With `operands` returning `WAT` twice
-- the exact refactor described above -- the build exits 1 instead of printing
`IDENTICAL -- 8287746 bytes`.

The deleted flag also restored generation 2 from generation ONE's bytes, which
was harmless only while the two agreed.

**The lesson is not "run the gate", it is "a check nobody has to remember is
worth more than a flag that proves the same thing on demand".**

## The Rust arm, repinned

**What this arm proves, and it is the only one that proves it:** an independent
front end, written in Rust, produces the same IR as the compiler does. It is
the only arm that sees defects ABOVE the IR wire, because zig and wasm both
inherit upstream's front end and a fixed point cannot see a frontend defect.

Oracles rebuilt at `8570fba1`; the port untouched at `f5686ea`.

    subject sha   ecfb2ff9af350393  ->  ab2af30213ac26f4
    codexzig      6512a08b0454f609  ->  65165823744c9838

### On the corpus, Update 57 moved NOTHING

`units-u56` is a materialised 1,246-unit corpus and none of the corpus gates
reads `CODEX_ROOT`, so the subject and the port both hold and exactly one
variable moves.

                      at e941ba11         at 8570fba1
    IR identical            865       ->        865
    IR differs              164       ->        164
    we-refuse                 3       ->          3
    oracle-refuses          204       ->        204
    both-refuse              10       ->         10
    counters agree            0       ->          0
    counters diverge       1032       ->       1032

**Bit-identical, both gates.** U57's compiler changes -- COMPILER-66's
comparator, CDX2097, the x86 over-application work -- move no unit's IR and no
unit's counters. The 0-agree divergence is entirely U56's `gen-eq-def`, and
implementing it remains the one thing that unblocks the counter gate.

That COMPILER-66 changes no emitted IR anywhere in 1,246 units is worth
recording on its own: it makes the `all-bindings` lookup deterministic without
moving a byte, which is what a repair of an ambiguity should look like.

### The self-host moved, and it is not attributable to this Update

    checkpoint pin    byte-identical 2845 of 2846   (1 differs: deck-record)
    at 8570fba1       byte-identical 2714 of 2869   (154 differs, 1 MISSING)

**The registered prediction was 2,846 of 2,846 and it is WRONG.** It was made
when PR 134's emitted half landed, and two Updates of compiler change have
gone by since; the prediction's other variables did not hold.

**But this number cannot be attributed to U57, and the reason is structural.**
The corpus gates hold `units-u56` fixed and repin only the oracle. Here the
subject IS the compiler, so a repin changes what is compiled and what grades it
in one step -- the subject went from 2,846 definitions to 2,869. There is no
`e941ba11` baseline for this gate because the earlier run measured only the two
corpus gates, and the old oracle is a build away.

Given the corpus is bit-identical across U56->U57, the move belongs to the
window that ends at U56, not to U57. Stated as attribution, not measurement.

### The step that reported PASS while comparing nothing

The self-host counters went off exact for the first time --

                     ours       oracle
    substitutions   119599      119985     +386
    next-id         119599      119985     +386
    expr-types      147700      147916     +216
    next-row-id     316077      316077        0
    check-errors         0           0        0

-- and the harness printed **PASS**. The step echoed both sides and never
compared them, so its exit code was `grep`'s. The same shape sat in the
checkpoint's chain, where the numbers happened to be equal and nobody looked.

**I copied both self-host steps verbatim into my own gate script and inherited
the defect rather than noticing it.**

Now `rust-codex-compiler/selfhost_gate.sh`, in the repo, in the README's gate
list, in `docs/gates.md`, comparing and exiting non-zero. It splits MISSING
from DIFFERS, which the old step did not, and that paid immediately: of the
155, exactly **one** is MISSING and it is `__eq_TokenKind` -- `gen-eq-def`'s
synthesised equality, reaching the wire here where the corpus prunes it.

`expr-types` moving is the same feature's other half. The signature recorded at
U56 was "+64 on substitutions and next-id, rows and `expr-types` UNCHANGED";
that was the synthesised definition alone. The instantiated helper for a
generic sum IS attached to the wire, and it carries spans.

### A refusal that is U56's, found by looking at a one-unit delta

Diagnostics went `agree 1076, differ 170` to `agree 1075, differ 171` -- but
the baseline was taken at the CHECKPOINT's oracle pin, not at `e941ba11`, so
that delta spans two oracle moves. `corpus_check_gate` was never re-run at
`e941ba11`.

The one unit is `unit-field-assign`, and the oracle now refuses it at the
LEXER, where it used to reach the type checker:

    ours   [1 CDX2095]        oracle [1 CDX8]

`CDX2095` is `FieldOnUnitType`, which is what the unit is for. `CDX8` is
`UnterminatedCharacter`. **U57 changed no lexer file and U56 changed
`Syntax/Lexer.codex`**, so this is U56-era, not something the repin introduced.

Reduced from the unit to a three-line chapter:

    3sp prose  "the compiler's own text"    ok     embedded apostrophe
    3sp prose  "don't and won't"            ok     embedded apostrophes
    3sp prose  "Balanced 'x' here."         ok     exactly one character
    3sp prose  "Empty '' here."             CDX8
    3sp prose  "Two chars 'ab' here."       CDX8

A whitespace-delimited `'...'` in PROSE that is not exactly one character halts
the compile with "Unterminated character literal: hit end of line before
closing `'`" -- while the closing quote is on the line. The first hypothesis,
"an apostrophe in prose", was wrong and a minimal repro said so.

It matters because upstream's own diagnostics quote names that way: the CDX2095
text is "`'Spot'` is a unit type ... has no field `'x'`". Pasting a diagnostic
into a chapter's prose breaks the build. One unit in 1,246 has the shape, which
is why only one moved.

## CORRECTION: the lexer refusal is OURS, and the corpus is two Updates stale

The entry above treats `unit-field-assign`'s CDX8 refusal as a U56-era upstream
defect and a PR candidate. **It is neither.** Working it up for submission is
what showed why.

`codex/test/errors/unit-field-assign.codex`, upstream, at three pins:

    675a0775  U55     "   CDX2060  ... '' is immutable"     3 spaces
    6cd2ca1b  U56     " CDX2060  ... '' is immutable"       1 space
    49fa9f27  U57     " CDX2060  ... '' is immutable"       1 space

    ~/units-u56/unit-field-assign.codex                     3 spaces

**Prose is one-space-indented and is not lexed. Upstream added the CDX8
diagnostic in U56 -- `Syntax/Lexer.codex` is the file U56 changed -- and
re-indented its own affected files in the SAME Update.** The diff between our
copy and theirs is exactly those two lines and nothing else.

`Lexer.codex` already carries the apostrophe rule and the census behind it: a
quote straight after a letter or a digit is an apostrophe and no diagnostic is
raised, measured over 4,025 files with zero false positives. `don't` and
`compiler's` pass for that reason. There is nothing here to send.

### The corpus named `units-u56` holds U55 sources

Of the 43 non-`apps/` test files that changed between U55 and U56, 19 are in
the corpus and comparable:

    corpus chapter matches U55    18
    corpus chapter matches U56     0
    matches neither                1

**Zero at U56.** The subject every corpus gate runs against is a U55-era cut
wearing a U56 name, and it has been graded against a U57 oracle all night.

That does not invalidate the ratchet -- holding the subject fixed while the
oracle moves is the right experiment, and it is why `identical 865` was
bit-identical across the repin. What it invalidates is the NAME, and any
reading of `oracle-refuses 204` as a property of upstream's current tree.
`unit-field-assign` is one refusal that exists only because our copy is stale;
there may be more.

**The fifth coordinate is not the only one that goes unlabelled.** A subject
pinned by a directory name nobody re-checked is the same failure as a bank
nobody re-took, and it was found the same way -- by trying to use the number
for something.

### What this costs and what it does not

Re-cutting the corpus at `8570fba1` moves the SUBJECT, so it resets the
ratchet: `identical 865` is a number about a U55 subject and cannot be compared
to a number about a U57 one. That is a deliberate act, like a rebank, and it is
not done here. `resolve_corpus.py` lives in the deprecated ladder and reads the
ambient `CODEX_ROOT`.

Until it is re-cut, quote the corpus numbers as what they are: **a U55-era
subject against a U57 oracle**, with the port between them.

